 ## Overview
-Optimized HAVS is a refinement of Hardware Assisted Visibility Sorting [Hardware Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS)](2918-hardware-assisted-visibility-sorting-havs.md) that maintains the k-buffer in a strictly sorted state (ascending order of fragment distance) to reduce the computational cost of fragment-level visibility sorting. While standard HAVS requires $2k-1$ comparisons to identify the two nearest fragments for compositing, this optimization reduces that requirement to $k$ comparisons per fragment processing step after the buffer is full, thereby improving rendering efficiency for large unstructured grid datasets.
+Optimized HAVS is a refinement of Hardware Assisted Visibility Sorting [Hardware Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS)](2918-hardware-assisted-visibility-sorting-havs.md) that maintains the k-buffer in a strictly sorted state (ascending order of fragment distance) to reduce the computational cost of fragment-level visibility sorting [S1]. While standard HAVS requires $2k-1$ comparisons to identify the two nearest fragments for compositing, this optimization reduces that requirement to $k$ comparisons per fragment processing step after the buffer is full, thereby improving rendering efficiency for large unstructured grid datasets [S1].
 
